<body>
<h1>Unit 1: Physical Quantities</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Before any law can be applied, a measurement has to be given a number, a unit, a direction where it matters, and an uncertainty. This unit fixes that vocabulary, and the 2019 SI redefinition moved the definition of the kilogram from a physical artefact to a fixed number.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>A physical quantity is a number multiplied by a unit; the unit is part of the quantity, not a label attached to it.</li><li>The SI defines seven base units (m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd); every other unit is derived from them by definition, so the unit system is a language in which equations must be well-formed.</li><li>Since the 2019 redefinition the metre is fixed by c = 299 792 458 m/s exactly, the kilogram by the Planck constant h = 6.62607015e-34 J s exactly, and the kelvin by k = 1.380649e-23 J/K exactly.</li><li>Addition and subtraction demand matching dimensions; multiplication and division create new dimensions, which is why dimensional analysis can reject an equation before you compute anything.</li><li>Every measurement carries uncertainty: the final reported digit is uncertain, and the honest form of a result is best estimate plus or minus a bound.</li><li>Order-of-magnitude estimation (10^n) is a cheap sanity check: if the answer of a units question is 10^40, something has already gone wrong.</li><li>Dimensional homogeneity is a consistency test rather than a proof: an equation can be dimensionally perfect and still be wrong by a factor of two, a wrong sign, or an unaccounted dimensionless constant.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Dimensional analysis only proves that an equation is dimensionally consistent, never that it is right; dimensionless constants such as pi, G and friction coefficients are invisible to the check.</li><li>The 'linear law' shape of Hooke's law is dimensionally consistent but only valid inside a small-strain regime, which is exactly the regime students assume without checking.</li><li>The modern kilogram is realised with Kibble and watt balances, so old textbooks that describe a platinum-iridium cylinder under lock and key are describing a definition that no longer holds.</li><li>Percentage errors are meaningful only for quantities measured on a ratio scale; a percentage change in temperature measured in Celsius is not comparable to the same change in kelvin.</li><li>The seven SI base units are a convention, not a discovery - a different set of definitions would describe the same physics, which is exactly why the 2019 redefinition changed the artefacts without changing a single law.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 2 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Precision and significant figures</li><li>Dimensions and uses of dimensional analysis</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A 2 kg body has a weight of 2 g, so the weight of a body tells you how much matter it contains.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Weight is a force and mass is a property of the body. The number 2 is the mass in kilograms; the weight is the force with which the body presses on a support, which is m times the local g.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Mass m = 2 kg is the amount of matter (and the measure of inertia). Weight W = m g = 2 x 9.8 = 19.6 N. On the Moon the mass is still 2 kg but the weight is about 3.2 N.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“If a formula is dimensionally correct, it must be physically correct.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Dimensional analysis only rejects a formula. A dimensionally consistent formula can still be wrong: v = k a t is dimensionally sound and still false for free fall.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Dimensional homogeneity is a necessary but not sufficient condition. A formula must first be dimensionally consistent, and then be derived or confirmed by experiment.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The number of significant figures is decided by the number of decimal places.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Significant figures and decimal places are different counts. 0.00450 m has two significant figures and three decimal places; 4500 m written without a decimal point has an ambiguous figure count.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Significant figures count the meaningful digits of a measurement, and the last one is the uncertain digit. In multiplication and division the answer takes the fewest significant figures of the operands; in addition and subtraction it takes the fewest decimal places.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A rod is measured as 4.56 m with an uncertainty of +/- 0.01 m. State the absolute and the percentage uncertainty, and the number of significant figures the result should be quoted to.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Absolute uncertainty = 0.01 m. Percentage uncertainty = (0.01 / 4.56) x 100 = 0.22 per cent. The reading has three significant figures (4, 5, 6), so the length is quoted as 4.56 m and the uncertainty as +/- 0.01 m.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>A fluid of viscosity eta flowing through a pipe exerts a retarding force F = eta A (dv/dx), where A is the cross-sectional area and dv/dx is the velocity gradient. Derive the dimensional formula of eta.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> From the equation, [eta] = [F] / ([A][dv/dx]) = (M L T^-2) / (L^2 x (L T^-1 / L)) = (M L T^-2) / (L T^-1) = M L^-1 T^-1. So [eta] = [M L^-1 T^-1], the dimensions of the poise (Pa s) or of the pascal-second.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A student writes that 'g = 9.8 m/s^2 is the weight of a 1 kg mass'. Explain, with the definition of the quantity, exactly what is wrong with the statement and what value is exact rather than measured.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> g is the acceleration of a body in free fall near the Earth's surface, a field strength, not a weight and not a force; it has units of m/s^2, whereas weight is a force in newtons. The weight of a 1 kg body is m g = 9.8 N. The value that is exact by definition is the standard gravity g_0 = 9.80665 m/s^2, fixed in the 1901 international convention; the measured local g differs from it because Earth's mass distribution and rotation make the field non-uniform.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Speed of light (exact)</td><td>299 792 458 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Planck constant (exact)</td><td>6.62607015e-34 J s</td></tr><tr><td>Boltzmann constant (exact)</td><td>1.380649e-23 J/K</td></tr><tr><td>Standard gravity</td><td>9.80665 m/s^2 (by definition)</td></tr><tr><td>Number of SI base units</td><td>7 (m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd)</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How small can the uncertainty of a realised kilogram become before metrology, rather than physics, sets the limit?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Quantum metrology keeps tightening traceability, but no fundamental floor on repeated mass comparison has been identified.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Should the SI keep seven base units, or fold constants such as the Boltzmann constant into a smaller set?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>The BIPM keeps this under periodic review; the debate is about definitional economy, not about new physics.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Express the speed of light, the Planck constant and the Boltzmann constant in base SI units and check that each is dimensionally M^0 L^0 T^0 Theta^0.</li><li>Your calculator reports g = 9.8 m/s^2. How many digits of that answer are trustworthy, and how would you justify your choice from the measurement alone?</li><li>Design a dimensional check that catches the classic mistake of adding a mass to a force.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/physical-quantities">Read the chapter notes: Physical Quantities</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-measurement">Measurement 3D lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-symbols-mechanics">Mechanics symbols reference</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/metric-si/si-units">NIST: SI Units</a></li><li><a href="https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Uncertainty/basic.html">NIST: Measurement uncertainty</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/1-4-dimensional-analysis">OpenStax: Dimensional Analysis</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/1-6-significant-figures">OpenStax: Significant Figures</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
